0ca3cd494246636a52599cd01c33d659a664e1c375a120","order":1,"original_content_hash":"sha256:51c0215ae925364fe50ca3cd494246636a52599cd01c33d659a664e1c375a120","parser":{"profile_hash":"sha256:812c184f0cf4bc1de8182dbf6380f241c9bef121a5bf80f2ec6d148009cd5949"},"physical_order":1,"raw_blob_id":"6805d8c4-4d74-5ad6-b133-0b81a2f3d09a","raw_file_hash":"sha256:c3788aa09a97c8df0dd2705e20c2149c5f122f390f45a66df9f789ec370da0c2","source_chapter_id":"body","source_locator":{"container_path":null,"field":"text","offset_kind":"HTML_SELECTOR","original_byte_offset":null,"path":"hydraulics-14.html","record":1,"selector":".mt-content-container"},"text":"A\ncheck valve\nis a simple yet essential component in hydraulic systems, designed to allow fluid flow in\none direction\nwhile\ncompletely blocking\nflow in the opposite direction. Because of this function, it is often referred to as a\none-way directional control valve\n.\nDespite their simplicity, check valves play a crucial role in many hydraulic applications. They ensure the proper operation of pumps, prevent system damage, and contribute to the overall efficiency of fluid power circuits. Some of the most common applications of check valves include:\nHolding Prime\n– In many hydraulic systems, especially those using\nlarger pumps\n, oil can drain from the\ninlet line\nwhen the system is idle for extended periods. This condition, known as\nlosing prime\n, occurs when the pump struggles to draw oil back into the system because atmospheric pressure alone is insufficient to push the fluid up the supply tube. A\ncheck valve\ninstalled in the inlet line prevents oil from draining out, ensuring the pump maintains its prime and is ready for operation when restarted.\nAvoiding Pump Reversal\n– In some hydraulic circuits, when the electric motor driving the pump is turned off,\nfluid pressure in the system can force oil backward through the pump\n. Many pumps are not designed to rotate in reverse and can suffer severe damage if this occurs. To prevent reverse flow, a check valve is placed at the\npump outlet\n, allowing flow to move forward while blocking any reverse movement.\nBypass Flow\n– Check valves are often used to create\nbypass pathways\naround specific components in a circuit. For example, a check valve may be placed in parallel with a\nflow control valve\nto regulate flow in one direction while allowing free flow in the other. This setup ensures controlled operation in one direction while\nminimizing restriction\nin the reverse direction.\nCreating Back Pressure\n– In some\ndirectional control valves (DCVs)\n, oil pressure is required to shift the spool. Certain valve designs require a\nminimum system pressure\nto ensure proper shifting. A check valve can be installed in the\ntank line\nto create\nback pressure\n, preventing pressure from dropping too low and ensuring reliable valve operation.\nBeyond these applications, check valves are also used to\nlock actuators in place\nand provide\nfree-flow pathways for hydraulic motors during deceleration\n. These functions will be explored in greater detail in later chapters.","title":"14.1: Check Valves in Hydraulic Systems","volume":"","work_id":"hydraulics-14","work_title":"hydraulics-14"}],"work_id":"hydraulics-14"}